  "track.runtime.m.records.st.merge": "On applying B",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.init.title": "Create count = mutableStateOf(0)",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.init.explain": "The state object `count` has one record, created in the global snapshot, with value 0. Anything reading `count.value` outside a snapshot of its own reads global.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.init": "Create count = mutableStateOf(0)",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.take.title": "Take two mutable snapshots A and B",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.take.explain": "Each snapshot is an isolated view of ALL state objects, captured when it is created. Both A and B read count = 0.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.take": "Take two mutable snapshots A and B",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.writeA.title": "A writes: count.value += 10",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.writeA.explain": "Inside `a.enter { }`, the write creates a new record tied to A's id, value 10. A reads 10; B and global still read 0, because A's record is not valid for them yet.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.writeA": "A writes: count.value += 10",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.writeB.title": "B writes count = {b}",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.writeB.explain": "B also creates its own record, tied to B's id. B knows nothing about A's record.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.writeB": "B writes count = {b}",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.applyA.title": "a.apply(): success, global = {g}",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.applyA.explain": "Since A was taken, nobody else changed count in global, so there is no conflict. A's change becomes global's change atomically. Apply observers receive the set {count}.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.applyA": "a.apply(): success, global = {g}",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.applyB.failed.title": "b.apply(): conflict, failure",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.applyB.failed.explain": "When B was taken, count was 0 (previous). Global is now 10 (current, from A). B wants 20 (applied). `structuralEqualityPolicy.equivalent(10, 20)` is false, and the default `merge` returns null: it cannot be merged. Apply returns Failure, and B's change does not reach global.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.applyB.failed": "b.apply(): conflict, failure",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.applyB.merged.title": "b.apply(): conflict, counterPolicy merges to 30",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.applyB.merged.explain": "It is still a conflict (previous 0, current 10, applied 20), but `counterPolicy.merge` returns `current + (applied − previous)` = 10 + 20 = 30. A new record with the merged value is written, and the apply succeeds.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.applyB.merged": "b.apply(): conflict, counterPolicy merges to 30",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.applyB.same.title": "b.apply(): equivalent values, success",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.applyB.same.explain": "B set 10, exactly the value A put into global. `equivalent(current 10, applied 10)` is true, so the runtime keeps current: nothing to merge, and the apply succeeds.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.applyB.same": "b.apply(): equivalent values, success",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.end.failed.title": "The end: global = 10, B's change is lost",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.end.failed.explain": "For a composition pass, this is when the runtime discards the result and schedules composition again with the new values.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.end.failed": "The end: global = 10, B's change is lost",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.end.merged.title": "The end: global = 30",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.end.merged.explain": "Both changes are kept because the policy knows how to merge. For \"counter\" state or a data class with several independent fields, merge is the way to avoid conflicts.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.end.merged": "The end: global = 30",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.end.same.title": "The end: global = 10",
  "track.runtime.m.records.s.end.same.explain": "Two snapshots arriving at the same value do not conflict.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.ev.end.same": "The end: global = 10",
  "track.runtime.m.records.co.readable": "At the pinned commit, the readable record is the valid record with the highest id: not above the reading snapshot's id and not in its set of invalid ids (which includes snapshots still open when it was created). [[src:rt-snapshot-src]]",
  "track.runtime.m.records.co.notify": "Applying a non-nested mutable snapshot also implicitly calls `sendApplyNotifications()`: observers registered with `registerApplyObserver` (the Recomposer is one) receive the set of changed state. [[src:rt-snapshot]]",
  "track.runtime.m.records.co.failed": "The reference says: if apply fails, the snapshot and the changes calculated during composition are disposed, and a new composition is scheduled. [[src:rt-snapshot]]",
  "track.runtime.m.records.co.merged": "`merge` is only called when current and applied are NOT equivalent. [[src:rt-policy]] [[src:rt-state-src]]",
  "track.runtime.m.records.co.end.failed": "Failure is normal and safe: no half-applied state leaks into global.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.co.end.merged": "A custom policy must be mathematically right: merge gets three values, not two.",
  "track.runtime.m.records.co.end.same": "This is also why the setter ignores equivalent values: assigning the same value again creates no change.",
  "track.runtime.cap.records": "Global on the middle left; A (top) and B (bottom) are taken from global and write their own records into count's record chain. Applying A brings its change back to global; applying B must go through the policy. The apply observer (the Recomposer, top left) receives the set of changed state to invalidate the scopes that read it.",
  "track.runtime.b4.sim.runtime.records": "Records, apply, merge",
  "track.runtime.b4.sim.runtime.snapshot": "Writes inside and outside a snapshot",
  "track.runtime.b4.quiz.q3.q": "Does assigning `title = title` (the same value) with the default policy cause recomposition?",
  "track.runtime.b4.quiz.q3.a": "No.",
  "track.runtime.b4.quiz.q3.why": "The setter checks `equivalent` before writing; an equal value is not written.",
  "track.runtime.b4.quiz.q4.q": "Is `derivedStateOf` needed for `val full = \"$first $last\"`?",
  "track.runtime.b4.quiz.q4.a": "No.",
  "track.runtime.b4.quiz.q4.why": "The result changes exactly as often as the inputs; derivedStateOf only adds overhead.",
  "track.runtime.m.survive.title": "Type \"Ana\", then... which state survives?",
  "track.runtime.m.survive.overview": "The user types \"Ana\" into a field, and the same text is held in five places: `remember`, `rememberSaveable`, a `StateFlow` in a ViewModel, the ViewModel's `SavedStateHandle`, and durable storage (DataStore/Room). Pick an event, then press Step to see which places still hold the text.",
  "track.runtime.m.survive.wrapup": "Each place that holds state is tied to a lifetime: `remember` lives with the Composition; `rememberSaveable` and `SavedStateHandle` go through saved instance state (a Bundle), so they survive configuration changes and system-initiated process death; a ViewModel lives in memory, so it survives configuration changes but not process death; only durable storage survives the user closing the app for good. Choose the holder by how long the state must live, and keep large data out of the Bundle.",
  "track.runtime.m.survive.simp.1": "The keep/lose table follows \"Save UI state in Compose\" and \"State and Jetpack Compose\"; it assumes no `android:configChanges` handling rotation manually, and that the screen is not removed from the Composition midway.",
  "track.runtime.m.survive.simp.2": "Durable storage only keeps what was FULLY WRITTEN to disk; the site does not model when the write happens. A draft in a text field usually does not need to survive the user closing the app: the docs consider losing transient state then reasonable.",
  "track.runtime.m.survive.ctl.hint": "Run the four events in turn and compare how many places still hold the text.",
